# =====================================================================# Urganize Web App (apps/web) — Environment Variables
# ============================================================================
# Copy to .env.local and fill in real values.
# Lines marked [required] must be set for the app to function.
# Lines marked [optional] have fallbacks or enable non-critical features.
# ============================================================================

# --- Supabase ---

# [required] Supabase project URL. Exposed to the browser for client SDK.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://your-project.supabase.co

# [required] Supabase anonymous (public) key. Exposed to the browser; RLS enforces access.
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.xxxxx

# [required] Supabase service-role key. Server-only — bypasses RLS.
# NEVER expose this to the client.
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY=eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.xxxxx

# --- App URL ---

# [optional] Canonical app URL. Falls back to VERCEL_PROJECT_PRODUCTION_URL,
# then VERCEL_URL (both set automatically by Vercel).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_URL=https://app.urganize.app

# --- Payments (Paystack) — recurring billing ---

# [required for payments] Paystack secret key. Used by:
#   - the webhook for HMAC signature validation
#   - /api/billing/start-checkout to initialize transactions
# Test mode uses sk_test_*, live mode sk_live_*. Swap this single value to
# move between Paystack test and live without code changes.
PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY=sk_test_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# [required for payments] Paystack plan codes (PLN_xxx). Create the plans
# in the Paystack dashboard (one plan per currency per cycle) and paste the
# resulting codes here. The app reads these at checkout time and on
# webhook events; nothing is hardcoded. Test-mode plan codes will start
# with PLN_test_; live-mode with PLN_live_ — the prefix is informational,
# the env var values are what matters.
#
# !! DISPLAY PRICE vs BILLED PRICE — check this on every reprice !!
# packages/pricing is the DISPLAY price; these Plan objects are what
# Paystack actually charges. As of the 2026-08-21 reprice the displayed
# team prices are NGN 80,000/mo and NGN 800,000/yr, but the Plans these two
# vars point at were minted at the old ₦150,000 / ₦1,500,000. Mint two new
# live Plans at the current prices and repoint these BEFORE switching the
# hard paywall on, or the first team to pay is overcharged. This gap has
# come up four times; see the comment block in packages/pricing/src/index.ts.
# STATUS 2026-08-22: closed for these two. Plans were minted at the displayed
# NGN 80,000 / 800,000 and these vars repointed BEFORE the paywall went on.
# Under pricing v2 these same two Plans serve the LABEL tier, whose prices are
# deliberately identical — do not repoint them when that flag flips.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_ANNUAL_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_USD=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_ANNUAL_USD=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
# ── TIER PLANS (pricing v2) — ANNUAL ONLY SINCE 2026-09-08 ───────────────────
#
# ⚠️ THESE ARE NEW VAR NAMES (_YEARLY_, not _ANNUAL_), AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.
# Nothing has them set, so shipping the code before the Plans exist means the
# tier is simply not offered on the wall rather than advertising 5,000 and
# charging 80,000. Reusing the _ANNUAL_ names would have made the order of
# operations load-bearing; this way it is not. The
# 2026-09-08 reprice moved every one of them (Solo 80,000 -> 5,000, Manager
# 350,000 -> 22,000, Label new at 50,000) and a Paystack Plan's amount is fixed
# when it is created, so a var still pointing at last week's Plan advertises
# 5,000 and charges 80,000. That gap has shipped five times in this repo.
#
# What makes forgetting survivable rather than expensive: purchasableTiers() in
# lib/billing-plans.ts withholds any tier whose code is unset, so an unminted
# tier is simply not offered on the wall. The failure mode is a missing card,
# never a wrong charge. Mint first anyway.
#
# EXISTING SUBSCRIBERS ARE NOT AFFECTED BY REPOINTING THESE. A Paystack
# subscription is bound to its Plan when it is created, so these vars decide what
# a NEW customer is charged and nothing about what an existing one pays. Do not
# delete the old Plan objects — deactivate them, so nobody new lands on one while
# the subscriptions already on them keep renewing.
#
# The monthly vars below are read ONLY by the renewal sanity check now
# (SUPERSEDED_TIER_PLAN_CODE_ENV_VARS), so a subscriber from before this reprice
# does not log a warning on every renewal. Leave them set; nothing sells against
# them.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_SOLO_YEARLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MANAGER_YEARLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
# ⚠️ NOT the same var as PAYSTACK_PLAN_LABEL_NGN four lines down. That one holds
# a mislabeled 750,000 Plan from an early attempt and is read by nothing on the
# tier path. They differ by one word; setting the wrong one bills a Label
# customer 750,000 for a 50,000 tier.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_LABEL_YEARLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
# Superseded Plans, read ONLY by the renewal sanity check. Never sold against.
# Leave whatever is already set here in place: it is what stops a subscriber from
# before the reprice logging a warning on every renewal.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_SOLO_ANNUAL_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MANAGER_ANNUAL_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_SOLO_MONTHLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_MANAGER_MONTHLY_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
# LEGACY (pre-tier) label plan, 750,000/yr. Read by nothing: checkout refuses the
# legacy `label` id outright because BILLING_PLANS still marks it contactSales.
PAYSTACK_PLAN_LABEL_NGN=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx
PAYSTACK_PLAN_LABEL_USD=PLN_xxxxxxxxxx

# --- Card-before-trial (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CARD_TRIAL) ---
#
# Server-only (not in turbo.json — request-time reads, the CHAPPI_API_KEY
# precedent). Both are only consulted when the flag below is on.

# [optional] Naira amount of the card-verification charge that tokenizes the
# card, refunded automatically by the webhook right after setup. Paystack
# cannot save a card for ₦0 (a real 2FA-authenticated charge is required) and
# its documented tokenization minimum is ₦50, so values below that are clamped
# up; anything unset/unparseable falls back to 100. NOTE: the page copy renders
# the ₦100 default from lib/trial-card.ts — update
# TRIAL_CARD_VERIFICATION_DISPLAY_NGN too if you override this.
# TRIAL_CARD_VERIFICATION_AMOUNT_NGN=100

# [required in prod when the flag is on] ISO timestamp. ONLY accounts created
# at or after this moment are bounced to /start-trial at login. This exists
# because the paywall has been off since 2026-07-23, so many EXISTING accounts
# sit at unpaid/never-trialed and must not suddenly be walled. UNSET = the
# login-time gate is inert (a warning is logged); onboarding still routes new
# signups to the trial page. Set it to the moment you flip the flag.
# TRIAL_CARD_SIGNUP_CUTOFF=2026-08-25T00:00:00Z

# --- AI ---

# [required for AI features] API key for the AI model gateway.
# If unset, AI features (rollout planner, status generator) are disabled.
AI_API_KEY=your-ai-gateway-api-key

# [optional] Base URL for the AI gateway. Defaults to the Zuplo gateway
# (Ollama-style protocol). Set to https://api.rodiumai.io to route via RodiumAi
# (OpenAI-style protocol; AI_API_KEY is then a RodiumAi rd_sk_… key, default
# model becomes openai/gpt-4o-mini). OpenRouter (https://openrouter.ai/api/v1)
# is also recognized (default openrouter/free). Override per feature with
# AI_PLANNER_MODEL / AI_STATUS_MODEL.
#
# Do NOT default to a RodiumAi meta/llama-* model, even though those are the
# free-tier option — confirmed 2026-08-13 against the live API that they're
# geo-blocked at the AWS Bedrock license level for this account's region
# ("Access to Meta Llama models is not allowed from unsupported countries").
# openai/gpt-4o-mini is the verified-working cheap choice instead.
# AI_API_BASE=https://api.rodiumai.io

# [optional] Model for the UrgAns rollout planner. Read at request time and
# resolved by lib/ai/planner-model.ts: unset on RodiumAi means openai/gpt-5.4,
# the strong OpenAI model, because the planner has to honour instructions like
# "no marketing" against a list of ~60-110 tasks and return a strict JSON
# delta, which the gateway's cheap default gets wrong in plausible-looking
# ways. Unset on any other gateway means that gateway's own default. Set it to
# pick a different model the gateway serves; openai/gpt-5.4-mini is the
# cheaper sibling if plans run close to the route's 60s ceiling (check the
# gateway's /v1/models before relying on it).
# AI_PLANNER_MODEL=openai/gpt-5.4

# [optional] How hard the planner's model thinks: none, minimal, low, medium
# or high. Default low, which holds the contract and keeps a plan well inside
# the route's ceiling. Sent only to models that accept the parameter (the GPT-5
# and o-series families); anything else never sees it. An unrecognised value
# falls back to low rather than failing every plan.
# AI_PLANNER_REASONING_EFFORT=low

# --- AI model router ---
#
# Server-only, so NOT in turbo.json (the CHAPPI_API_KEY precedent): read at
# request time, so changing any of these is an env edit and a redeploy, never a
# code change. See lib/ai/model-router.ts, model-ladder.ts and routed.ts.
#
# [optional] Routes each AI request to the cheapest model that suits it, from
# today's openai/gpt-4o-mini up to the GPT-5.6 family, and moves a request to a
# sibling or another rung when a model fails.
#   unset / anything else   OFF. Every request goes exactly where it always did.
#   shadow                  Decides and RECORDS what it would have chosen (a log
#                           line, and metadata on the urgans_action billing
#                           event) but every request still uses today's model.
#                           Turn this on first and read it before going live.
#   on                      Routes for real.
# Only these exact words count: "true", "1" and "yes" leave it off on purpose, so
# a habit from the NEXT_PUBLIC_ flags cannot switch on something that changes
# what every request costs. It is also off whatever it says on a gateway that is
# not RodiumAi (CHAPPI_API_BASE for UrgAns chat, AI_API_BASE for the rest),
# because the ladder below is a list of RodiumAi catalogue ids.
# AI_ROUTER=shadow
#
# [optional] The four rungs, cheapest first. Each is a comma list of catalogue
# ids, PRIMARY FIRST; the rest are same-tier siblings the router falls to on a
# rate limit or an outage before it moves a rung. At most four ids per rung, each
# `vendor/name`. Defaults, chosen 2026-09-29 from GET https://api.rodiumai.io/
# v1/models with the founder:
#   quick     openai/gpt-4o-mini, openai/gpt-5.6-luna
#   standard  openai/gpt-5.4-mini, openai/gpt-5.6-luna
#   pro       openai/gpt-5.4, openai/gpt-5.6-terra
#   max       openai/gpt-5.5, openai/gpt-5.6-sol     (the `gpt-5.6` alias is Sol)
# For UrgAns chat the quick rung's primary is CHAPPI_MODEL (today's model is the
# floor) unless AI_LADDER_QUICK is set. Terra and Sol are limited by the provider
# to 10 requests a minute and 500 a day, which is why each has a sibling with
# three times the room.
# AI_LADDER_QUICK=openai/gpt-4o-mini,openai/gpt-5.6-luna
# AI_LADDER_STANDARD=openai/gpt-5.4-mini,openai/gpt-5.6-luna
# AI_LADDER_PRO=openai/gpt-5.4,openai/gpt-5.6-terra
# AI_LADDER_MAX=openai/gpt-5.5,openai/gpt-5.6-sol
#
# [optional] Reasoning effort per rung: none, minimal, low, medium, high, or off
# (never send it). Default low. Sent only to models that accept it (the GPT-5 and
# o-series); anything else never sees it. A value the models would reject falls
# back to the default.
# AI_LADDER_QUICK_EFFORT=low
# AI_LADDER_STANDARD_EFFORT=low
# AI_LADDER_PRO_EFFORT=low
# AI_LADDER_MAX_EFFORT=low
#
# [optional] The highest rung a team's plan lets a request climb to: quick,
# standard, pro or max. Only a team that is actually PAYING gets its plan's
# ceiling; a trial, a comped team, a grace period and an unknown team are held at
# AI_ROUTER_CEILING_TRIAL. (`tier` alone cannot be used: it reads as the uncapped
# flagship for a trial, so a cap read from it would give every 3-day trial the
# most expensive model.) A surface's own floor beats the ceiling: the rollout
# planner never runs below pro.
#   AI_ROUTER_CEILING_SOLO      default standard
#   AI_ROUTER_CEILING_MANAGER   default pro
#   AI_ROUTER_CEILING_LABEL     default max   (also every legacy plan)
#   AI_ROUTER_CEILING_TRIAL     default pro
#
# A pinned model turns routing OFF for that feature: AI_PLANNER_MODEL,
# AI_STATUS_MODEL, AI_OUTCOME_MODEL and AI_PRESSKIT_MODEL each keep meaning "use
# exactly this", so an existing pin stays a pin. UrgAns chat has no pin: set
# AI_ROUTER=off to hold it on CHAPPI_MODEL.
#
# Measuring before switching on: lib/ai/model-eval.integration.test.ts asks the
# provider (opt-in, needs a key, costs a few dollars) whether it accepts the
# request shapes the router sends and how often each model's first move is
# right. Its header has the commands. It never runs in `pnpm test`.
#
# Reading a shadow run, on the production database (routing rides on the same
# billing event as the monthly count, so nothing else changes):
#   select metadata->'routing'->>'start_tier' as wanted,
#          count(*), avg((metadata->'routing'->>'tokens_in')::int) as avg_in,
#          avg((metadata->'routing'->>'latency_ms')::int) as avg_ms
#   from billing_events
#   where type = 'urgans_action' and metadata ? 'routing'
#   group by 1 order by 1;

# --- Admin ---

# [optional] Comma-separated list of admin email addresses.
# Required for /api/admin/* routes to function.
# ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com,ops@example.com

# [optional] Neon connection string for the MARKETING site's database (the
# same DATABASE_URL the homepage app uses). Powers the admin hub's Waitlist
# and Growth dashboards (/admin/waitlist, /admin/growth), which were ported
# in from apps/homepage. When unset, only those two pages error — everything
# else in the app is unaffected.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://user:pass@host/db

# --- Cron ---

# [required for scheduled jobs] Shared secret for /api/cron/* routes, which
# have no user session to authenticate against. Set this in Vercel's project
# env vars and Vercel automatically sends it as `Authorization: Bearer
# <value>` when triggering the cron defined in apps/web/vercel.json — no
# other wiring needed. Generate with `openssl rand -hex 32` or similar.
# CRON_SECRET=

# --- MCP access (per-user, over OAuth) ---

# [optional] NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_MCP_CONNECT — lets EVERY user connect their own
# LLM client (claude.ai, Claude Desktop, ChatGPT, Claude Code, Cursor) to their
# own workspace and take their release context with them. Default off; off, every
# OAuth route and both /.well-known documents 404, and /api/mcp serves only the
# founder key below.
#
# Why OAuth and not a per-user token: claude.ai web and Claude Desktop assume
# OAuth 2.1 and attempt Dynamic Client Registration on connect, with no "no auth"
# option and no static-header fallback. A token would have worked for Claude Code
# and for nobody else. Tokens and codes live in mcp_oauth_* (migration
# 20260825100000_mcp_oauth.sql) and are stored hashed.
#
# Needs no other env: the app is its own authorization server. Users revoke a
# connection in Settings -> Account -> Connected apps.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_MCP_CONNECT=false

# --- MCP access (founder-only) ---

# [optional] Bearer secret + bound account for /api/mcp, which exposes Chappi's
# tool surface to an external MCP client (Claude Code / Claude Desktop). BOTH
# must be set or the endpoint 404s — presence of the pair is the feature
# switch for THIS path, so it stays dark by default. This key is bound to one
# account: a caller holding MCP_ACCESS_KEY acts as MCP_USER_ID and nobody else.
# Per-user access is the separate, flag-gated OAuth path above, and the two are
# independent — either, both or neither may be switched on.
#
# MCP_ACCESS_KEY — generate with `openssl rand -hex 32`. Passed by the client as
#   `Authorization: Bearer <value>`; add the server with:
#     claude mcp add --transport http urganize \
#       https://app.urganize.app/api/mcp --header "Authorization: Bearer <value>"
# MCP_USER_ID — the auth.users id every MCP call acts as (the founder's).
#
# Rotating = replacing the value here. This key has no revocation UI, which is
# one of the reasons per-user OAuth exists: those connections are listed and
# revocable in Settings -> Account -> Connected apps.
# MCP_ACCESS_KEY=
# MCP_USER_ID=

# --- Web Push notifications ---

# [required for push notifications] VAPID key pair identifying this app to
# the browser push services. Generate ONCE with:
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# then set the same pair in Vercel (Web project) and never rotate casually —
# rotating invalidates every existing device subscription. The public key is
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ because the browser needs it to subscribe; the private key is
# server-only. When unset, the app runs fine with push silently disabled
# (the Settings section hides itself and sends become no-ops).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=

# [optional] Contact for push-service operators (defaults to
# mailto:contact@urganize.app).
# WEB_PUSH_CONTACT=mailto:contact@urganize.app

# --- Native push notifications (FCM) ---

# [required for native push] Full Firebase service-account JSON, pasted as
# one line — see docs/mobile/push-setup.md. Server-only, deliberately not
# in turbo.json's env allowlist (same as CRON_SECRET). Reaches the
# Expo native shell (apps/mobile), which has no PushManager and so
# cannot use the Web Push keys above. When unset, native sends silently
# no-op (Web Push, if configured, keeps working).
# FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON=

# --- Analytics (PostHog) ---

# [optional] PostHog project API key (browser-side). If unset, analytics disabled.
# Set either NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY or NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=phc_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_PROJECT_TOKEN=phc_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# [optional] PostHog API host. Defaults to https://app.posthog.com.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://app.posthog.com

# --- Analytics (Vercel) ---

# [optional] Set to "true" to enable Vercel Analytics. Disabled by default.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ENABLE_VERCEL_ANALYTICS=true

# --- Feature flags ---

# [optional] UrgAns release planner. When 'true', a rollout-plan release is
# created with no activities: the manager answers a short intake, reviews and
# edits the draft plan UrgAns tailors from the hand-crafted playbook, and
# approving it is what creates the tasks. Works with no model configured (the
# playbook plan becomes the draft), so this can be switched on before the
# gateway is routed. Supersedes the removed tailor-plan feature, which applied
# model output straight onto real task rows.
#
# Requires the release_plan_drafts migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260810110000_release_plan_drafts.sql).
# Do NOT enable alongside NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FIRST_WIN: that reveal celebrates
# a generated plan, and under the planner a new release has none yet.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden; the plan API routes 404).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RELEASE_PLANNER=false

# [optional] UrgAns in every function, Phase 0 + "Draft the plan" (spec 6.1).
# One tap on a release drafts its rollout plan through the existing planner
# machinery above, gated to Manager/Label/Group (Solo sees a locked sheet with
# an upgrade prompt), logged to a new urgans_actions table, and capped at 100
# runs per team per rolling 24h (a runaway-usage backstop, not a per-plan
# number). Nothing writes to `tasks` until the manager taps Apply.
#
# Requires BOTH this flag AND NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RELEASE_PLANNER — the
# embedded action is worthless without a planner to draft into. Also requires
# the urgans_actions migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260903100000_urgans_actions.sql).
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden; the new /api/urgans/actions routes 404).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_URGANS_ACTIONS=false

# [optional] EP and album playbooks. When 'true', the EP (ep_v0.1, 16 weeks
# around a lead single at -42) and album (album_v0.1, 21 weeks around two
# staggered singles at -84 and -42) templates become selectable: the onboarding
# step-0 picker enables them, the create-release modal grows a type picker, and
# the UrgAns planner drafts EP and album plans from their own playbook instead
# of falling back to the single one.
#
# Both templates are v0.1 DRAFTS. They have not had the two design-partner
# review passes single_v0.3 went through, which is the only reason this flag
# exists — the code path is the same one single has always used.
#
# No migration needed: the release_type DB enum already accepts 'ep' and
# 'album'. Defaults to 'false' (EP/album show as "Coming soon" in onboarding,
# no type picker in the modal, planner drafts from the single playbook).
#
# LIVE IN PRODUCTION as of 2026-08-14 (v0.55.0). Note what it took: setting the
# Vercel env var was NOT enough on its own. The version-bump commit that
# accompanied the flip touched only package.json files and packages/changelog,
# which apps/web does not depend on, so `npx turbo-ignore @urganize/web` skipped
# the web build entirely — the marketing site rebuilt and the product app did
# not, leaving the env var set but never inlined. NEXT_PUBLIC_* is baked into
# the client bundle at build time, so flipping one of these flags requires a
# commit that actually touches apps/web.
#
# To check whether it is live without an account: GET /onboarding and count
# "Coming soon" in the HTML. Three means off (ep + album + music_video are all
# gated); one means on (only music_video is).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_EP_ALBUM_TEMPLATES=false

# [optional] AI status update generator ("Generate status update"). Defaults to
# 'false' (hidden; the API route 404s). Set to 'true' once Anthropic API key is
# configured and Zuplo gateway routes to Claude Haiku for status updates.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_STATUS_UPDATE=false

# [required in prod] Consolidated workspace view at /dashboard. PRODUCTION
# DEFAULT IS 'true' — the two-pane workspace (releases column + tasks panel)
# is the live experience. Leave unset locally to fall back to the LegacyDashboard
# component (kept in app/dashboard/page.tsx as a one-line revert path). Set to
# 'false' to explicitly disable in any environment (the LegacyDashboard renders
# at /dashboard). The legacy /dashboard/releases and /dashboard/tasks routes
# 308-redirect to /dashboard regardless of this flag.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_WORKSPACE=true

# [optional] Chappi conversational AI front door — a full-width hero at the top
# of the workspace. Phase 1 (this default) is a deterministic command bar that
# drives existing app actions and works with the AI provider OFF, so it is safe
# to enable in dev/preview immediately. The conversational agent turns on once
# CHAPPI_MODEL + CHAPPI_API_KEY are set (Phase 4). Only renders when
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_WORKSPACE is also on. Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CHAPPI=false

# [optional] UrgAns chat history. When 'true' (and NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CHAPPI is
# on), UrgAns chats are saved: in the browser for an instant reload, and in the
# urgans_conversations table so they follow the user to other devices. Reopening
# UrgAns resumes a chat touched in the last six hours; older chats live in a
# history list on the UrgAns page and in the side rail, where they can be
# reopened or deleted. Private to their owner. Requires the
# urgans_conversations migration to be applied first (the data export reads the
# table even with this flag off). With the flag off, every
# /api/urgans/conversations route 404s. Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_URGANS_HISTORY=false

# [optional] Voice input for UrgAns. When 'true' (and NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CHAPPI
# is on), the chat composer grows a mic button: the person records a short clip,
# it is transcribed server-side, and the text lands in the message box for them
# to fix and send (nothing is sent automatically, and the audio is never
# stored). Requires URGANS_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL to be set. Gates the mic and the
# /api/urgans/transcribe route (404 when off). Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_URGANS_VOICE=false

# [optional] Release content calendar (URG-157). When 'true', each release gains
# a "Content" section (calendar list) and a /dashboard/releases/[id]/calendar
# page for planning social posts per platform account. Planning only, no
# posting. Requires the content_calendar migration to be applied to the
# database first; with the flag off, all content-calendar API routes 404.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CONTENT_CALENDAR=false

# [optional] "First win" moment (URG-107). When 'true', a new user's FIRST
# release creation triggers a celebratory reveal of the auto-generated rollout
# plan + a (skippable) teammate invite, instead of navigating straight to the
# release. Purely additive UX; safe to enable. Defaults to 'false'.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FIRST_WIN=false

# [optional] Release file/asset storage (URG-182). Gates the Asset section on
# the release detail page, the standalone /dashboard/releases/[id]/files page,
# the workspace "Files" link, and the assets API routes (404 when off). This
# turns the feature OFF, it does not delete anything: existing rows in the
# `assets` table and objects in the `release-assets` bucket are untouched, and
# flipping this back to 'true' restores access to them. Defaults to 'false'
# (hidden) as of URG-182 — was previously always on with no flag.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FILES=false

# [optional] Song upload. When 'true', each release gains a "Song" scene where
# the owner uploads the audio master, plays it back, hands it over and removes
# it — and UrgAns can start that upload from the conversation (the `get_song`
# and `upload_song` tools, plus a typed "upload the song" that works with no
# model configured). Gates the pill, the ?scene=song URL param, the /song API
# routes (404 when off) and the assistant's upload card.
#
# NO MIGRATION: a song is an `assets` row with category 'audio' in the existing
# private release-assets bucket, so the storage model, RLS and per-release
# cleanup already cover it.
#
# Deliberately SEPARATE from NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FILES above, even though they
# share that storage: the file cabinet was switched off in URG-182, and "attach
# the master to this release" is a different ask from "bring the file manager
# back". Either can be on without the other. Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_SONG_UPLOAD=false

# [optional] Events: shows, setlists and DJ packs. When 'true', an artist gains
# a second kind of object beside releases — a show, with a run of show, one or
# more setlists measured against the slot length, and a DJ pack sent out as an
# unguessable per-recipient link with read receipts.
#
# NEEDS A MIGRATION: supabase/migrations/20260830120000_events.sql (nine tables
# plus four enums). Apply it before flipping this, or every events route errors.
#
# The largest sanctioned scope expansion so far, and the only one that moves
# three lines off the out-of-scope list at once — see the migration header and
# CLAUDE.md. Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_EVENTS=false

# [optional] Cover art. When 'true', each release gains a "Cover art" scene
# where the owner uploads/replaces/removes the release's cover image — shown
# on its workspace card, the press kit, and anywhere else the release is
# displayed. Uploading a new image REPLACES the current one (the previous
# asset is deleted, unlike Song, which keeps every version) — the whole point
# of this scene is "the image I just chose is now the cover." Gates the pill,
# the ?scene=coverart URL param, and the /cover-art API routes (404 when off).
#
# NO MIGRATION: cover art is an `assets` row with category 'cover_art' in the
# existing private release-assets bucket, and `releases.artwork_asset_id`
# already exists (it has powered the release cards' cover art since Phase 3).
# This scene is the first dedicated surface for setting it directly, rather
# than relying on the general Files scene's one-time auto-promote-on-first-
# upload behavior.
#
# Deliberately SEPARATE from NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FILES above, same reasoning
# as Song upload: the file cabinet was switched off in URG-182, and "update
# this release's cover image" is a different, more central ask than "bring
# the file manager back". Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_COVER_ART=false

# [optional] Timeline view. When 'true', a scoped release gains a "Timeline"
# pill that lays every activity out on a week axis anchored to release day —
# phases collapsible, a today line, and drag to reschedule at wide widths.
#
# NO API ROUTES AND NO MIGRATION. The scene is a lens on activities the
# workspace already holds, so this flag gates a pill, a `?scene=timeline` URL
# param and nothing else. Durations come from `metadata.template.duration_days`,
# which template instantiation has written on every generated activity since it
# was first built.
#
# Phase 1 of the spec: one release, read plus drag. The roster view (every
# active release on one axis) and UrgAns rescheduling are Phase 2.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TIMELINE=false

# [optional] Mobile bottom navigation. When 'true', below lg the topbar's nav
# icons (Catalog/UrgAns, Notes) and the "New release" CTA move into a fixed
# bottom tab bar (Home / UrgAns / New release / Notes / You), and the
# ChappiDock floating launcher retires on that same range (the bar's UrgAns
# tab replaces it; its WhatsApp founder link moves to a footer row on the
# /dashboard/urgans page). Purely additive chrome; no migration, no data
# change. Defaults to 'false' (hidden; topbar and dock render exactly as
# before).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_MOBILE_NAV=false

# [optional] Soft, dismissible banner steering Android browser visitors
# (detected via lib/platform.ts's isAndroidBrowser(), a UA sniff — never true
# inside the installed native app) toward apps/homepage's /download page.
# Purely additive chrome; no migration, no data change. Defaults to 'false'
# (hidden; no banner anywhere).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_ANDROID_APP_BANNER=false

# [optional] Offline support. When 'true', the app registers an offline
# service worker (public/sw-offline.js, replacing the push-only public/sw.js
# at the same registration scope) that caches static assets and the app
# shell, so a dropped connection shows the cached catalog and chrome instead
# of a blank browser page — and, for surfaces that queue (activity
# creation, task completion, notes, cover art / song uploads), lets the
# user keep working offline and syncs when the connection returns. Reads,
# uploads that don't queue (files, metadata edits, calendar posts), and
# every server-side feature (auth, AI, payments, admin) are unaffected —
# see the "Offline support" section in CLAUDE.md for the full analysis of
# what works offline, what queues, and what stays online-only. Defaults to
# 'false' (the service worker stays push-only, exactly as before; no fetch
# interception, no caching, no queue).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_OFFLINE=false

# [optional] Type-first release creation (founder call, 2026-09-01). When
# 'true', the create-release modal becomes ONE screen: tap Single / EP /
# Album and the release is created on the spot — no plan chooser (every
# type-first release is a rollout release; "start blank" is covered by never
# setting a date and adding your own activities), no title, no date. The tap
# lands straight in the release (no team-login access step, no first-win
# reveal, no ?new=1 guide), and the name + release date are prompted later:
# the undismissable SetReleaseDateCard in the activities panel from the
# start, plus a one-time bottom sheet (ReleaseSetupSheet) that slides up
# after the user has explored a little — first scroll, first scene open, or
# ~10 seconds, whichever comes first.
#
# NO MIGRATION: title and date were already optional at POST /api/releases
# (URG-230), and the dateless rollout release reuses the existing
# template_pending + /schedule machinery unchanged.
#
# Do NOT enable together with NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RELEASE_PLANNER (its
# intake modal would intercept the straight-into-the-release landing) or
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FIRST_WIN (it celebrates a plan a dateless release
# does not have yet). Defaults to 'false' (the two-step modal, the access
# step and the ?new=1 guide all behave exactly as before).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TYPE_FIRST_CREATE=false

# [optional] Old EPK links (URG-196, RETIRED 2026-09-05). The release-scoped
# press kit was replaced by the standing per-artist one
# (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PRESS_KITS). Nothing in the app makes an EPK link any
# more: the scene, the API routes and the UrgAns tools are all gone.
#
# This flag now has ONE job: whether an old /epk/[token] link still resolves.
# Those links may be sitting in press inboxes, so the page is kept as a
# compatibility shim. It renders the EPK exactly as it always did until that
# artist's press kit is PUBLISHED, and then 307s to /k/[slug].
#
# LEAVE THIS ON. Turning it off breaks every link anyone was ever sent, and is
# the last step of the retirement rather than part of it.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_EPK=true

# [optional] Fan email capture (2026-08-14). Conscious v1 scope expansion
# (see CLAUDE.md). When 'true', each release gains a "Fan capture" scene: the
# owner authors one public page (graphic, headline, incentive, destination
# link) at /capture/[token], a fan gives an email and the link is revealed on
# the same screen, and the collected emails come back in-app for export. The
# hand-crafted conversion playbook in lib/capture-playbook grades the page as
# it is written. Storage and display only — nothing sends email, and the
# destination is a plain URL the manager types (no DSP, no pre-save API).
# Requires the release_capture_pages migration to be applied first; with the
# flag off, the capture API routes 404 and /capture/[token] 404s.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CAPTURE=false

# [optional] Team logins (founder request, 2026-08-16). When 'true', two
# things switch on: (1) creating a release opens a compulsory "get your team
# in" step that auto-generates a sign-in link for that release, and (2)
# Settings -> Team gains a "Team logins" section where a manager generates as
# many links as they need, copies each to WhatsApp or email, and revokes any
# of them. A login is a LINK, never a password — whoever opens it chooses
# their own password on /invite/accept, so nothing shareable is ever a
# readable credential.
# Builds on the invite-link machinery that has shipped since 2026-07-23; the
# mint endpoint (POST /api/workspace/invite-link) stays live either way,
# because Settings -> Teammates already calls it. This flag gates the LIST and
# REVOKE endpoints, the new section, and the post-create step.
# Requires the workspace_invite_links team-logins migration
# (20260816100000) for labels, release provenance and the revoke audit
# column; without it links still mint, they just lose that context.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TEAM_LOGINS=false

# [optional] Card before free trial (founder request, 2026-08-18). When
# 'true', a new signup must add a card before reaching the app: onboarding
# lands them on /start-trial, they pick Monthly or Annual, and Paystack takes
# a small refundable card check (see TRIAL_CARD_VERIFICATION_AMOUNT_NGN). The
# webhook then saves the reusable card authorization, creates a Paystack
# subscription whose start_date is the trial end, refunds the check, and marks
# the account trialing — so the card is charged automatically when the 28 days
# lapse, unless cancelled from Settings -> Billing.
# Also switches off the no-card path: POST /api/trial/start 404s (closing the
# one-POST bypass), onboarding stops granting a trial, and /locked's trial
# button routes to /start-trial instead.
# Scope is NEW SIGNUPS only — the login-time bounce additionally requires
# TRIAL_CARD_SIGNUP_CUTOFF (above), and never touches existing accounts,
# invited teammates or curators. The server paywall stays off either way.
# Before flipping this in production: mint NGN Paystack Plans that match the
# CURRENT displayed prices and repoint PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_NGN /
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_ANNUAL_NGN — the plans in use still bill the pre-2026-08-16
# amounts, and that is what a converting trial would charge.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CARD_TRIAL=false

# --- Team trial + hard paywall (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_HARD_PAYWALL) ---
# [optional] The team trial and the wall at the end of it (2026-08-21).
# SUPERSEDES the card-before-trial flow above: that one takes a card before the
# app and charges at day 28; this one gives a team 3 days of full access with
# no card, then walls until they pay or talk to us. Do not run both.
#
# When 'true':
#   - every team gets 3 days from creation (shortened from 14 by a founder call on 2026-09-10) (stamped at provisioning; existing
#     teams were stamped by 20260821120000_team_trial_hard_paywall.sql, which
#     is what starts their clock)
#   - past the trial with no paid owner and no comp, every protected route
#     returns 402 and the app renders /locked. Nothing is deleted; access comes
#     back the moment the team pays, is comped, or is extended
#   - every member of a team inherits the owner's access, so an invited
#     teammate is never asked to pay for a team they were invited into
#   - the owner or an admin sees the plan picker and a direct line to the
#     founder; everyone else is told their admin needs to activate
#   - a trial indicator appears at 10 days left or fewer
#   - /admin/teams can extend, comp, assign Founding Member, set a custom
#     price, or mark a team paid for deals settled by invoice
#   - POST /api/trial/start 404s (it would otherwise be a one-POST bypass)
# Curator-only accounts are never walled, and /admin stays reachable so a
# walled founder can still comp a team.
#
# REQUIRES the migration above to have been applied. Before flipping this in
# production, mint Paystack Plans at the CURRENT displayed prices and repoint
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_NGN / PAYSTACK_PLAN_ANNUAL_NGN — see the warning beside
# those vars. Founder alerts additionally need BILLING_ALERTS_EMAIL (below).
# Defaults to 'false' (the app stays free, exactly as today).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_HARD_PAYWALL=false

# --- Pricing tiers (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PRICING_V2) ---
# Switches billing from one team price to a ladder of tiers, each paid once a
# year: Solo (NGN 5,000), Manager (NGN 22,000), Label (NGN 50,000) and Group
# (sales-led, quoted per contract, no self-serve checkout). Adds the per-plan
# entitlement map in lib/entitlements.ts, which is the first thing in this
# product that maps a plan to what it can DO rather than just whether it has
# access.
#
# ANNUAL ONLY SINCE 2026-09-08, and the amounts above are that reprice. There is
# no monthly option on any tier: the interval toggle is gone from both the
# public page and the in-app wall, and a checkout request naming `monthly` is
# refused with a 400. BillingInterval still carries 'monthly' because real
# subscriptions bill on it and still renew; what ended is selling one.
#
# ALL THREE PRICED TIERS NEED THEIR OWN PLAN OBJECT at the new amounts — see the
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_*_ANNUAL_NGN block above, and mint them FIRST. Label used to
# reuse the shared legacy annual Plan because its price was identical to it;
# at 50,000 it is not, so that reuse would now bill 800,000.
#
# REQUIRES two migrations:
#   20260823100000_pricing_tiers.sql            (widens subscriptions_plan_check
#                                                for the tier ids, adds
#                                                workspaces.legacy_rate_expires_at)
#   20260824100000_subscriptions_billing_interval.sql
#                                               (adds subscriptions.billing_
#                                                interval — WITHOUT IT a tier
#                                                subscription cannot record
#                                                whether it bills monthly or
#                                                annually, and renewals extend
#                                                by the wrong period)
#
# WHAT THIS FLAG NOW GATES. Checkout accepts a tier + interval, the webhook
# records the interval and renews on it, and Settings names a tier plan. The
# caps (seats, active releases, assistant actions) are NOT gated by this flag at
# all — they are always on and structurally inert until a team actually resolves
# to a capped tier, which only a tier subscription can cause.
#
# ALSO GATED, as of 2026-08-24: the public three-column /pricing page (in
# apps/homepage — SET THIS ON BOTH VERCEL PROJECTS, or the site advertises tiers
# the app cannot sell, or the reverse) and the in-app tier picker on the paywall.
#
# ALSO GATED, as of 2026-08-24: plan changes and proration
# (POST /api/billing/change-plan). REQUIRES
# 20260824110000_subscriptions_pending_plan_change.sql. Note that a plan change
# leaves you a MANUAL Paystack swap to perform — there is no plan-change endpoint
# — and the deadline is the team's expires_at. Watch for the "SWAP NEEDED" alert
# and the SWAP OWED line on /admin/teams.
#
# STILL SEPARATE WORK: read-only-on-payment-failure.
# Defaults to 'false'.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PRICING_V2=false

# --- Data export (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_DATA_EXPORT) ---
# Adds "Your data" to Settings -> Account, directly above Delete account, with
# two downloads: everything as JSON, and every activity as a spreadsheet CSV.
# Backed by GET /api/account/export[?format=csv], which 404s when this is off.
#
# THE ROUTE IS DELIBERATELY NOT BEHIND THE PAYWALL, and that is not an
# oversight to tidy up. lib/entitlements.ts declares data_export on every tier
# with a test enforcing it: the people who most need their data out are exactly
# the ones a paywall would stop (a walled team, a failed card, a cancellation
# inside its 90-day window). It uses getAuthenticatedUser, never
# requireActiveAuthenticatedUser.
#
# Scope is the account-deletion boundary — you can export exactly what deleting
# your account would destroy. No migration, no new table; every read is
# select('*') so a column added later is exported without anyone remembering.
# Defaults to 'false'.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_DATA_EXPORT=false

# --- Issue reporting to Linear (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_REPORT_ISSUE) ---
# [optional] When 'true', users can report a bug or make a request from inside
# the app and each one becomes a Linear issue. Two entry points: an item in the
# `?` HelpMenu, and a section in Settings -> Account (which is what makes it
# reachable on a phone at all — the help menu is `hidden md:block`).
#
# Gates the dialog, both entry points, and POST /api/issue-reports, which 404s
# when off. /admin/issue-reports is deliberately NOT gated, so a report that
# failed to reach Linear stays visible either way.
#
# THE ROUTE IS DELIBERATELY NOT BEHIND THE PAYWALL, the same call
# /api/account/export makes and for a stronger reason: a walled or read-only
# team must be able to tell us something is broken. It uses
# getAuthenticatedUser, never requireActiveAuthenticatedUser, and
# lib/issue-reports-route-guard.test.ts reads the route source and fails if any
# paywall wrapper appears in it.
#
# A report is SAVED FIRST and the Linear push is a follow-up, so with the three
# vars below unset the feature still works — reports land in `issue_reports`
# with status 'failed' and are retryable from /admin/issue-reports.
# Defaults to 'false'.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_REPORT_ISSUE=false

# [required for the Linear push] A Linear personal API key, from Linear's
# Settings -> Security & access -> API. Sent in the Authorization header RAW,
# with NO "Bearer" prefix — a personal `lin_api_…` key is passed directly, and
# prefixing it fails auth with a 401 that looks exactly like a bad key.
#
# Server-only, and deliberately NOT in turbo.json's env allowlist (the
# CHAPPI_API_KEY / RESEND_API_KEY / GENRE_PREDICTION_URL precedent) so it can
# never reach a client bundle. Only the NEXT_PUBLIC_ flag above belongs there.
# LINEAR_API_KEY=lin_api_...

# [required for the Linear push] The UUID of the Linear team new issues land
# in. Both this and LINEAR_API_KEY must be set — either one alone leaves the
# push switched off.
# LINEAR_TEAM_ID=

# [optional] Label ids applied by report kind. Unset means the issue is filed
# with no label rather than the wrong one.
# LINEAR_BUG_LABEL_ID=
# LINEAR_REQUEST_LABEL_ID=

# [optional] Release metadata hub + collaborator credit capture (URG-108).
# Third conscious v1 scope expansion (see CLAUDE.md). When 'true', each
# release gains a "Metadata" section: an editable distribution-metadata form
# (ISRC, UPC, genre, language, explicit, label, (P)/(C) lines) plus a
# shareable, no-login link at /credits/[token] where collaborators submit
# publishing/split-sheet credits (legal name, roles, songwriter/master
# splits, PRO/IPI, publisher, socials). Submissions are capture + display
# only — no automation, no signing. Requires the release_credits_capture
# migration to be applied first; with the flag off, the credits API routes
# 404 and /credits/[token] 404s.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_METADATA=false

# [optional] Task attachments (URG-203). Gates the "Attachments" section in
# the task detail pop-up and the two task-attachment API routes (404 when
# off). Distinct from NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_FILES, which is release-level file
# storage. Turns the feature OFF, it does not delete anything: existing rows
# in `task_attachments` and objects in the `task-attachments` bucket are
# untouched, and flipping this back to 'true' restores access to them.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden) as of URG-203 — was previously always on with
# no flag.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TASK_ATTACHMENTS=false

# [optional] Workspace identity card (URG-214). When 'true', a compact
# LinkedIn-company-page-style card (logo, workspace/label name, artist name,
# teammate + active-release counts) renders in a persistent left column
# across the dashboard, UrgAns, Settings, and per-release scene pages.
# Display-only — edits happen in Settings. Only meaningful when
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_WORKSPACE is also 'true'; gates the identity panel, the
# Settings "Workspace identity" section, and the /api/workspace/identity +
# /api/workspace (PATCH) routes (404 when off).
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_IDENTITY_PANEL=false

# [optional] Genre prediction from audio. When 'true', the Metadata scene's
# Genre field gains a "Predict from audio" action: the user picks a local
# audio file (4 MiB max), it is proxied server-side to an external genre
# classifier, and the predicted genre prefills the field for review before
# saving. The audio is never stored and nothing is written until the user
# saves. Only meaningful when NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_METADATA is also 'true';
# requires GENRE_PREDICTION_URL. Gates the control and the
# /api/releases/[releaseId]/metadata/predict-genre route (404 when off).
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_GENRE_PREDICTION=false

# [optional] Notes: private, owner-scoped quick capture at /dashboard/notes,
# with an optional link to a release. Gates the topbar icon, the workspace
# identity panel's nav entry, the scoped-release "Notes" link in the tasks
# panel, the page itself (bounces to /dashboard when off), and all four
# /api/notes routes (404 when off). Requires the notes migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260808120000_notes.sql) to be applied first.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_NOTES=false

# [optional] Task outcomes ("Done. How did it go?"). When 'true', marking a
# task done opens an UrgAns bottom sheet asking how it went: three outcome
# chips, an optional note, and at most one AI follow-up question when a note
# was written. Outcomes land in the task_outcomes table and show read-only on
# the task detail pop-up. Per-user "Ask me after tasks" toggle in Settings
# (on by default) disables the sheet. Works with AI off: summaries fall back
# to templates and no follow-up is asked. The AI call uses AI_OUTCOME_MODEL,
# falling back to AI_STATUS_MODEL, then the gateway default, with a 3 second
# budget that fails silently. Requires the task_outcomes migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260809120000_task_outcomes.sql) to be applied first;
# with the flag off, the /api/tasks/[id]/outcome route 404s.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TASK_OUTCOMES=false

# Team brief (Phase 2 of the UrgAns shared-context build): one standing
# description of the team — roster, how they work, positioning, constraints,
# vocabulary — injected into every UrgAns request. Default off: the API route
# 404s, the Settings editor does not render, and nothing is injected.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TEAM_BRIEF=false

# [optional] Playlist pitching. When 'true', teams get a "Pitch" scene on a
# scoped release for browsing curator playlists and sending a song, and invited
# curators get their own surface at /curator (inbox, playlists, profile) with no
# access to any team data. Curator access is invite-only: an admin mints
# single-use links at /admin/curator-invites. Gates the workspace pill, the
# /curator pages (which bounce when off), and every /api/curator,
# /api/pitching and /api/admin/curator-invites route (404 when off). Requires
# the pitching migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260810100000_playlist_pitching.sql) to be applied
# first, since it also widens the profiles.role check to admit 'curator'.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PITCHING=false

# [optional] A playlist curator's public page at /pitch/{slug}. When 'true', a
# curator can publish one link that shows her name, her bio and her playlists,
# and whose call to action sends an artist into signup and then straight to the
# pitch composer scoped to her. READ ONLY: nothing on that page writes a pitch,
# so it opens no submission surface and needs no rate limiting. Gates the public
# page (404 when off), the "Your public page" block on /curator/profile, the
# publish routes, and the curator filter on the browse route. Only meaningful
# alongside NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PITCHING, which it also requires: a public page
# advertising playlists she cannot log in to manage would outlive the feature it
# belongs to. Requires
# supabase/migrations/20260915100000_curator_public_page.sql to be applied
# first, which adds the slug and published_at columns it reads.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CURATOR_PAGE=false

# [optional] Curator pitch payments. When 'true', an artist team can pay a
# curator's own price for a pitch instead of a free one: a listen and a
# written reply inside the response window, never a playlist spot. One
# Paystack transaction per checkout, split so each curator's share goes
# straight to her verified bank account (via a Paystack subaccount) and the
# Urganize fee goes to Urganize. A curator who never adds a price or a bank
# account keeps taking free pitches (opt-in, per curator); Solo teams keep
# paying with no plan gate.
#
# Requires BOTH NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PITCHING (this is a layer on top of
# pitching, not a replacement for the free path) and
# supabase/migrations/20260925130000_curator_pitch_payments.sql applied first.
#
# Gates: the price/bank-account sections on /curator/earnings, the paid-pitch
# checkout flow on the artist side, the /admin/pitch-payments console, and
# every /api/pitching/{quote,checkout,payments}, /api/curator/{pricing,payouts,
# earnings} and /api/admin/pitch-payments route (404 when off). NOT gated: the
# webhook's paid-pitch branch, the two cron sweeps, and the paid-reply rules on
# a pitch already paid for — those follow the ROW, not the flag, because a real
# payment must keep settling and a real refund must keep happening even if this
# is switched off after the fact.
#
# PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY (below) must be set for any of this to charge, split, or
# refund anything; with it unset the quote/checkout routes answer 503 rather
# than 404, so the feature can be switched on for internal testing before a
# live key exists.
#
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden). Ship this OFF everywhere until Paystack's
# dynamic multi-split and subaccount KYC questions (see lib/paystack-server.ts)
# are confirmed against a real test-mode account.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PITCH_PAYMENTS=false

# [optional] Which shape of Paystack split curator pitch payments uses.
# 'dynamic' (the default) sends an inline flat split on every
# /transaction/initialize call — no split object to create or maintain, and it
# is what the code was written against. 'split_code' instead creates one
# Paystack Split for the checkout and initializes against its code; only worth
# it if a future account setup or Paystack conversation makes dynamic splits
# unavailable. See lib/paystack-server.ts's paystackSplitMode().
# PAYSTACK_SPLIT_MODE=dynamic

# [optional] Native push notifications. When 'true', the Expo native
# shell (apps/mobile) registers for FCM push and Settings' push section
# renders the native variant instead of hiding (a native webview has no
# PushManager, so the Web Push variant never applies there). Requires
# FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON to be set for sends to actually go out — see
# docs/mobile/push-setup.md — and the device_push_tokens migration
# (supabase/migrations/20260810130000_device_push_tokens.sql) to be applied
# first. Release reminders, weekly motivational nudges, and deadline-tier
# copy are editable at /admin/notifications regardless of this flag; this
# flag only gates the NATIVE transport and its client UI — Web Push (browser
# subscriptions) is unaffected either way.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_NATIVE_PUSH=false

# [optional] First-login push prompt. When 'true', a signed-in user whose
# device has never been asked sees a small dismissible card (bottom-right,
# below every modal) offering to turn notifications on for that device — the
# same enablePush/enableNativePush flow the Settings row runs, just put in
# front of people instead of waiting to be found. It only ever appears where
# a prompt is actually possible: never once the browser permission is denied
# (browsers refuse a second prompt), never on an already-subscribed device,
# and a "Not now" holds for 30 days (PUSH_PROMPT_SNOOZE_MS in
# lib/push-prompt.ts). A device whose permission is already granted but whose
# subscription went missing is repaired silently, with no card at all.
# Requires NEXT_PUBLIC_WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY + WEB_PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY
# for browsers, and additionally NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_NATIVE_PUSH +
# FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_JSON inside the native shell — with neither transport
# configured the card correctly never shows, because there is no push to
# enable. No migration.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PUSH_PROMPT=false

# [optional] "Put us on your Home Screen" prompt (founder request,
# 2026-08-30; retriggered and widened 2026-08-31). When 'true', a signed-in
# visitor is offered the install a minute into every visit, on a small card at
# the bottom of the screen where a thumb reaches. Three platforms, three
# mechanisms: iOS gets the Add to Home Screen steps, because no API on any
# page can open that sheet; Android gets a link to the real app on
# apps/homepage's /download page; a DESKTOP browser gets the browser's own
# install, and only once it has actually offered us one (so desktop Safari and
# Firefox see nothing rather than a button that cannot work). Never shown
# inside the installed native shell or in an already-installed Home Screen app.
#
# ONCE PER VISIT, and the two answers are stored differently ON PURPOSE. A
# "Not now" holds for the browser session only, so the next visit asks again —
# that is the founder's "every time". Taking the install is durable for 180
# days (INSTALL_PROMPT_ACCEPTED_MS in lib/install-prompt.ts), because someone
# who already installed must not be asked tomorrow, and on Android nothing the
# page can read will ever confirm the APK install for us.
#
# TWO KNOCK-ON EFFECTS, both intended. It SUPERSEDES
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_ANDROID_APP_BANNER: with this on, that top banner never
# renders, because both point Android at the same /download page. And it
# RESEQUENCES NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PUSH_PROMPT on devices where an install is
# on offer — the notification ask holds until this card is answered, then
# follows only a "Not now". On iOS that ordering is forced rather than chosen:
# Apple exposes the Notification API only to Home Screen apps, so there is no
# permission to ask for in a Safari tab. No migration.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden; the Android banner and the push ask behave
# exactly as they do today).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_INSTALL_PROMPT=false

# [optional] Guided tours (founder request, 2026-09-09): two short
# Bitwarden-style walkthroughs — a spotlight cutout plus an anchored card,
# "1 of 3", Next/Skip — that show a new manager where things are instead of
# leaving them to find out.
#
# TOUR A (catalog) runs ONCE per account, unasked, on the first visit to
# /dashboard in a session. Note that is not the same as the landing page: with
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_CHAPPI on, a signed-in user lands on UrgAns and reaches
# the catalog by tapping, so the tour waits for them there. Its last step is
# contextual — plan the scoped rollout, create the first one, or open one —
# so it hands off into real work rather than ending on a shrug.
#
# TOUR B (rollout) is OFFERED, never auto-run: an inline card after Approve
# plan, which is the moment a rollout becomes a rollout. It replaces the
# NewReleaseGuide banner while this flag is on; with it off that banner is
# unchanged.
#
# Both are replayable from the `?` help menu (md+) and Settings → Account
# (which is what makes replay reachable on a phone). A running or requested
# tour also holds back the ~30s first-release prompt, so the two never talk
# over each other. No migration, no new table: "seen" is one localStorage key
# per account per tour.
# Defaults to 'false' (no tour ever starts; the data-tour attributes the
# anchors carry are inert).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TOURS=false

# [optional] The test path (founder request, 2026-09-01): someone who just
# wants to look around can create a ready-made TEST release in one tap, no
# form — from the zero-release empty state's "Try a test release" button or
# the first-release pop-up's "Just looking?" link. The release is titled
# "Test release", dated 9 weeks out with the full single rollout plan, marked
# with the `test_release` metadata key, and carries a standing banner whose
# "Delete test release" button is the path's exit. Test releases skip the
# come-back push reminders. With this off, the entry points and banner never
# render and POST /api/releases refuses `isTest` with a 400. No migration.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TEST_RELEASE=false

# [optional] Textile character layer. When 'true', <html> carries `urg-textile`
# and the app picks up the woven-cloth ornament layer: an ambient warp/weft/twill
# grain replacing the flat 36px graph-paper grid, a double-rule cartouche frame
# with corner ticks, Adinkra tooth borders on section rules and empty states,
# and gold-thread sunburst grounds on the entrances (auth, onboarding, locked),
# the public share pages (EPK, credits, capture) and the first-release reveal.
#
# Every ornament colour derives from EXISTING tokens (--color-stroke and
# --color-status-warning) at low alpha, so nothing new enters the palette and
# both themes are covered by the same cascade. Ornament sits at z-index 0 behind
# `.app-layout` (z-index 1), so it never renders over text.
#
# CSS-only — no migration, no new dependency, no raster assets, and the static
# prerendering of /auth and /onboarding is unaffected. With this off the app is
# byte-identical to before the layer existed, so reverting is an env var rather
# than a deploy. Note NEXT_PUBLIC_* is inlined at build time: flipping this needs
# a deploy that actually rebuilds apps/web.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_TEXTILE=false

# --- Row-level-security tests (development only) ---

# [optional, dev only] Points lib/pitching-rls.integration.test.ts at a real
# development Supabase project so it can prove the pitching isolation rules with
# actual PostgREST calls as signed-in users. With these unset the whole suite
# skips, so `pnpm test` stays offline and green. NEVER point these at
# production: the tests sign in as real accounts and attempt writes.
# Full setup steps are in that test file's header.
# RLS_TEST_SUPABASE_URL=
# RLS_TEST_ANON_KEY=
# RLS_TEST_TEAM_A_EMAIL=
# RLS_TEST_TEAM_A_PASSWORD=
# RLS_TEST_TEAM_B_EMAIL=
# RLS_TEST_TEAM_B_PASSWORD=
# RLS_TEST_CURATOR_1_EMAIL=
# RLS_TEST_CURATOR_1_PASSWORD=
# RLS_TEST_CURATOR_2_EMAIL=
# RLS_TEST_CURATOR_2_PASSWORD=
# RLS_TEST_PITCH_ID=
# RLS_TEST_CURATOR_1_USER_ID=

# [optional] Release recap card. When 'true', a scoped release whose date has
# passed (or whose every task is done) gets a "Get your release recap" action
# that builds a shareable poster: two PNGs (1080x1920 story and 1080x1080
# square) rendered server-side from the release's own numbers, stored in the
# release-assets bucket and shared through the native share sheet on mobile or
# downloaded on the web. One optional input, a highlight, is entered by hand.
# Playlist pitch counts appear on the card only when the pitching feature's
# table is present, and are omitted silently otherwise. Requires the recap
# migrations (supabase/migrations/20260810090000_release_recaps.sql and
# 20260810120000_release_recaps_drop_first_week_streams.sql) to be applied
# first; with the flag off, the /api/releases/[releaseId]/recap route 404s
# and no action is shown.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RELEASE_RECAP=false

# [required for genre prediction] Full endpoint URL of the external genre
# model's predict endpoint (a FastAPI service on an AWS Lambda function URL,
# owned by a collaborating developer). Server-only on purpose: the upstream
# has no authentication today, so the URL must never reach a client bundle.
# When unset, the predict-genre route returns 503.
# GENRE_PREDICTION_URL=https://<function-id>.lambda-url.us-east-1.on.aws/predict

# [optional] Shared secret forwarded to the genre model as an x-api-key
# header. Pre-wired for when the model's owner adds authentication; ignored
# upstream until then, so it is harmless to set early.
# GENRE_PREDICTION_API_KEY=

# [optional] Chappi conversational backend (Phase 2+). CHAPPI_MODEL is a
# tool-capable catalogue id on the RodiumAi gateway — openai/gpt-4o-mini is
# verified working (real tool_calls, correct arguments, tested 2026-08-13).
# Do NOT use a meta/llama-* model here: RodiumAi's free tier is all Meta/AWS
# Bedrock, and Bedrock's Meta license blocks unsupported countries/regions —
# confirmed live against this account, not a code bug. CHAPPI_API_KEY is the
# RodiumAi key (rd_sk_…), kept separate from AI_API_KEY so the two transports
# have independent credentials. CHAPPI_API_BASE overrides the gateway base URL
# (defaults to https://api.rodiumai.io/v1); the endpoint must speak OpenAI
# chat completions with tools/tool_calls.
# CHAPPI_MODEL=openai/gpt-4o-mini
# CHAPPI_API_KEY=
# CHAPPI_API_BASE=https://api.rodiumai.io/v1

# [optional] Voice input transcription model (NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_URGANS_VOICE).
# Server-only, so NOT in turbo.json (the CHAPPI_API_KEY precedent). The
# /api/urgans/transcribe route posts the clip to CHAPPI_API_BASE/audio/
# transcriptions with this model id, reusing CHAPPI_API_KEY — one vendor, no new
# credentials. Set to a RodiumAi transcription model id (e.g. a whisper or
# gpt-4o-transcribe variant) ONCE that endpoint is confirmed to exist on the
# account. Unset, the route 503s and the mic reports voice is unavailable.
# URGANS_TRANSCRIBE_MODEL=

# ============================================================================
# Transactional email (Resend) — OPTIONAL
# ============================================================================
# Powers every email the app sends: invites, the welcome email (or the
# onboarding sequence, below), assignment notes, credit submissions, founder
# alerts, and whatever is written in /admin/emails/new. Everything in this
# section is read server-side only, so it is deliberately NOT in turbo.json's
# env allowlist (the CRON_SECRET / CHAPPI_API_KEY precedent).
#
# Unset, lib/email.ts no-ops silently: inviting a teammate still creates the
# row and returns a copyable single-use link, and the UI says the link was
# created rather than claiming an email was sent. Nothing breaks — email is
# strictly additive.
#
# RESEND_API_KEY — from resend.com/api-keys. Sending permission is enough.
# EMAIL_FROM     — must be on a domain VERIFIED in Resend, or every send is
#                  rejected. NO MAILBOX IS REQUIRED at this address: Resend
#                  authenticates by DNS (DKIM + SPF), so any address on a
#                  verified domain is a legal sender.
#
#                  Verifying the ROOT domain does NOT mean touching the root
#                  SPF record that Namecheap Private Email relies on. Resend's
#                  Return-Path defaults to send.<domain>, so its SPF lands on
#                  that subdomain and its DKIM on resend._domainkey — both new
#                  hostnames, neither colliding with the root SPF or root MX.
#                  Check the dashboard's Records tab: if any record's Host is
#                  bare @ with a v=spf1 value, that one has to be MERGED into
#                  the existing root SPF (one SPF record per domain, always) —
#                  never added as a second record.
#
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO — optional, and unnecessary when EMAIL_FROM is already a real
#                  mailbox: replies go there by themselves. Set it only when
#                  sending from an address nobody reads (e.g. a no-reply or a
#                  send-only subdomain), to point replies at a human.
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# EMAIL_FROM=Lucky at Urganize <lucky@urganize.app>
# EMAIL_REPLY_TO=

# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET — the signing secret of the webhook in the Resend
#                  dashboard (Webhooks, pointed at /api/webhooks/resend). It is
#                  how bounces and spam complaints reach email_suppressions, so
#                  nobody who bounced or complained is emailed again. Unset, the
#                  route answers 404 and delivery events are not recorded.
# EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET — optional. Signs the unsubscribe links. Unset, the
#                  secret is derived from RESEND_API_KEY. EITHER WAY, CHANGING
#                  THE SECRET BREAKS EVERY UNSUBSCRIBE LINK ALREADY SENT, and
#                  while this is unset, rotating RESEND_API_KEY changes it. So
#                  before rotating the key, set this to the secret the key
#                  currently derives (readUnsubscribeSecret in
#                  lib/email/unsubscribe.ts) and old links keep working.
# RESEND_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# EMAIL_UNSUBSCRIBE_SECRET=

# EMAIL_ONBOARDING_SEQUENCE — the five getting-started emails a new signup
#                  gets over their first week (days 1, 2, 3, 5 and 7; copy in
#                  lib/email/onboarding-sequence.ts). Only the literal word "on"
#                  switches it on; "true" and "1" deliberately do not. Off, a
#                  new signup gets the one welcome email exactly as before. On,
#                  day 1 goes out at signup (in place of the welcome email) and
#                  days 2 to 7 wait in email_outbox for the morning cron.
#                  Switching it off again PAUSES the queued ones: nothing is
#                  deleted, they just are not sent until it is back on. A step
#                  more than 4 days late is dropped rather than sent out of
#                  order. Needs supabase/migrations/20260930120000_email_outbox.sql.
#                  Read at request time, so a change needs a redeploy (Vercel
#                  snapshots env per deployment) but no code change.
# EMAIL_ONBOARDING_SEQUENCE=on

# EMAIL_DAILY_LIMIT — how many emails the Resend plan allows per day, across
#                  everything. Defaults to 100, the free plan. Queued mail (the
#                  sequence and admin emails) only uses what is left after a
#                  reserve: the sequence keeps 10 back and an admin email keeps
#                  25 back, so a large send to everyone can never stop a
#                  teammate's invite from going out. What does not fit today
#                  goes out on the next morning's cron. Raise this after
#                  upgrading the Resend plan.
# EMAIL_DAILY_LIMIT=100

# BILLING_ALERTS_EMAIL — where the founder's billing alerts land (team entering
#                  its last 3 trial days, first time a team hits the wall, a
#                  walled team coming back, someone asking to talk, a team
#                  paying). Server-only, so deliberately NOT in turbo.json.
#                  Unset means alerts are silent: every event is still written
#                  to billing_events and still shows in /admin/teams, only the
#                  email is skipped. Requires RESEND_API_KEY + EMAIL_FROM above
#                  and NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_HARD_PAYWALL to be on.
# BILLING_ALERTS_EMAIL=lucky@urganize.app

# ============================================================================
# Removed in the recurring billing migration (2026-06-05)
# ============================================================================
# The following env vars are no longer read by the app:
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_PAYSTACK_PUBLIC_KEY  — was used by client-side <PaystackButton>;
#                                    the inline checkout flow has been replaced
#                                    by server-initiated /api/billing/start-checkout
#                                    which uses PAYSTACK_SECRET_KEY only.
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_CODE       — renamed to PAYSTACK_PLAN_MONTHLY_NGN
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_YEARLY_CODE        — renamed to PAYSTACK_PLAN_ANNUAL_NGN
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_GROUP_*            — group billing dropped from v1 surface
# PAYSTACK_PLAN_8MONTHS_CODE       — legacy plan, removed
# TRIAL_TOKENIZATION_AMOUNT_NGN    — card-on-file trial removed
# TRIAL_CLAIMS_SALT                — card-on-file trial removed
# SUPABASE_URL                     — replaced by NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL

# [optional] Press kits. When 'true', each artist gets one standing press kit
# with one permanent public link, replacing the release-scoped EPK. Gates the
# side menu entry, the topbar icon, the sixth tab in the mobile bar, the search
# catalog entry, /dashboard/press-kits (which bounces to /dashboard when off),
# and every /api/press-kits route (404 when off).
#
# NEEDS A MIGRATION: supabase/migrations/20260905100000_press_kits.sql (five
# tables). Apply it before flipping this, or every press kit route errors.
#
# Publishing a kit puts it at /k/[slug], and an old /epk/[token] link for that
# artist starts handing over to it at that moment. Until a kit is published its
# public page 404s and old EPK links keep rendering the EPK.
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_PRESS_KITS=false

# [optional] The Urganize Directory (2026-09-05). An in-app record of the
# people who do the work on a release: managers search for someone who has
# delivered the role their rollout needs, and professionals opt in to being
# found.
#
# Nothing here is self-reported. A person claims a role; the product proves it
# from task history, using lib/delivery-records.ts. There is no write path from
# any client to the delivery_records table, which is what makes the record
# worth anything.
#
# Requires supabase/migrations/20260905200000_person_directory.sql.
#
# PHASE 1 ONLY as of this writing: schema, the role map and the backfill exist;
# there is no profile screen, no directory search and no role gaps panel yet.
# Turning this on today changes nothing a user can see.
#
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_DIRECTORY=false

# [optional] Share to socials (founder request, 2026-09-23). Turns a rollout
# into three things the team posts from their own phone: an Instagram post
# image (1080x1350), a WhatsApp status image (1080x1920) and a message to DM
# anybody, with a caption for each.
#
# The sheet opens BY ITSELF once, right after a person's first real rollout
# (test rollouts never count), then every rollout carries a Share button and
# UrgAns can open it ("post it on instagram"). It always asks four questions
# first: who is posting, whether the date can be public, a link for fans, and
# one extra line.
#
# Nothing is posted by Urganize and nothing is stored on the server: the images
# are rendered on request by /api/releases/[releaseId]/share-card, answers are
# remembered per rollout in this browser only. No migration.
#
# Gates the sheet, the Share button, the first-rollout offer, the UrgAns tool
# and typed command, and the share-card route (404 when off).
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden). X and Snapchat tabs (2026-09-24) ride on this
# flag too; they reuse the same two images.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_SOCIAL_SHARE=false

# [optional] Brand colors for share images (founder call, 2026-09-24). Each
# artist gets a background and an accent in Settings -> Profile, and their
# share images use them instead of Urganize's colors. The text color is picked
# for contrast, and an accent too close to its background is kept off the
# words, so no pair of colors can make an image unreadable.
#
# Needs supabase/migrations/20260924120000_artists_brand_colors.sql (two
# nullable columns on artists). Safe before the migration: every read falls
# back to Urganize's colors, only the Settings save would fail.
#
# Gates the Settings section, the share sheet's "Set brand colors" line, the
# brandColors field on GET /api/workspace/artists, the image route reading the
# colors, and PUT /api/workspace/artists/[artistId]/brand-colors (404 when off).
# Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_BRAND_COLORS=false

# [optional] Home tab, step 2 of its build (mapping report + plan approved
# 2026-09; not yet reviewed on a real account). A new /dashboard/home landing
# page answering "what needs me right now?" — built for the manager,
# mid-release state only. Every other role/state combination renders an
# honest "not built yet" placeholder rather than invented data; the rest ship
# in later steps.
#
# One request (GET /api/home) returns the same releases/tasks the catalog
# loads plus Home-only data: shows in the next 14 days with DJ-link receipts,
# and recent pitches. Reuses the catalog's own visibility rules
# (loadWorkspaceAccessContext, decideReleaseAccess) — no new access model.
#
# Gates: the /dashboard/home route, HOME_PATH (lib/home-path.ts — takes
# precedence over the assistant as the post-auth landing when both are on),
# the topbar logo's destination, the side menu's Home row, the mobile bar's
# Home tab, and the page-search entry. Defaults to 'false' — with it off,
# every one of those behaves exactly as it does today.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_HOME=false

# [optional] Release chat (Release & Task Chat, Phase 1). When 'true', a
# scoped release gains a "Chat" scene: a thread visible to anyone with release
# access, timestamped and attributed, oldest-first. Sits beside the task
# thread already living inside every activity's pop-up (that one is unflagged
# — its backend, task_comments, has shipped since 2026-05 and this pass only
# resurrected the UI for it, narrower than general task visibility; see
# hasTaskThreadAccess in lib/tasks-server.ts).
#
# NEEDS A MIGRATION: supabase/migrations/20260929100000_release_messages.sql
# (one table, structurally identical to task_comments). Apply it before
# flipping this, or every release-chat route errors.
#
# Gates the pill, the ?scene=chat URL param, and the /messages API routes
# (404 when off). Defaults to 'false' (hidden).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_FEATURE_RELEASE_CHAT=false
